@* ColorPicker's Overview, written by hand after TextField's: swatches, the panel's parts, saving a
   color (previewed in each mode), empty values, the browser's picker, states and its own words. Accessibility and See also come from the doc
   comment. *@

<Heading Level="2">Usage</Heading>
<Code Block Language="razor">@Usage</Code>
<TextBlock>A color picker is a field for choosing a color: a swatch and a hex box. The swatch opens a <Anchor Href="docs/components/color-panel">ColorPanel</Anchor> in a popover, with swatches, a picker and fields; changes apply at once. Its value is a hex string such as <Code>#2563eb</Code>.</TextBlock>
<TextBlock>Underneath is a native color input, so the field posts with the form and opens the browser's own picker <Anchor Href="docs/common/render-modes">with JavaScript off</Anchor>. To show the colors without a popover, use a ColorPanel.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Swatches</Heading>
<TextBlock>The panel offers 21 colors by default. Set <Code>Swatches</Code> to offer your own, and <Code>SwatchText</Code> to name them: screen readers hear "Teal" rather than "dark cyan", and the name is the swatch's tooltip. An empty list shows none.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="color-picker-swatches" />
<TextBlock>The user can still pick or type any color. If the value must be one of the swatches, check it on the server.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">The panel's parts</Heading>
<TextBlock>The panel's parts are ColorPanel's, and each shows or hides on its own:</TextBlock>
<List>
    <ListItem><Code>ShowPicker</Code> and <Code>ShowFields</Code> (both true by default) show the picker and the hex and RGB fields. Set both to false for swatches only.</ListItem>
    <ListItem><Code>ShowAlpha</Code> adds an opacity slider and field; a color that isn't opaque becomes <Code>#rrggbbaa</Code>.</ListItem>
    <ListItem><Code>Clearable</Code> adds a swatch for no color, which makes the value null.</ListItem>
    <ListItem><Code>ShowEyedropper</Code> adds a button that picks a color from the screen, in browsers that have one.</ListItem>
</List>

<Heading Level="2">Saving a color</Heading>
<TextBlock>The picker binds to a string, in lowercase: <Code>#F60</Code> becomes <Code>#ff6600</Code>. This example offers swatches only.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="color-picker-label-color" />
<List>
    <ListItem>The hex box accepts three, six or (with <Code>ShowAlpha</Code>) eight hex digits, with or without the <Code>#</Code>. Anything else is put back to the last valid value when the box loses focus.</ListItem>
    <ListItem>On static pages, the property gets the posted text as it is, and a crafted form post can send any text. Before you save or use it, pass it through <Code>ColorValues.NormalizeHex</Code>, which returns <Code>#rrggbb</Code> or null, as this example does.</ListItem>
</List>

<Heading Level="2">Empty values</Heading>
<TextBlock>A property that's null or not a color shows an empty swatch, and stays null until the user picks one. With JavaScript off, the browser's color input has no empty value: it shows black, and the form posts <Code>#000000</Code>.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">The browser's picker</Heading>
<TextBlock>To keep the browser's own picker, set <Code>UseNative</Code>. It looks different in each browser and has no opacity. Swatches show under the field, and Chrome and Edge also list them in their picker.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Sizes, swatch only and locked</Heading>
<TextBlock>To line the picker up with other fields, set <Code>Size</Code>, and to show the swatch alone, set <Code>ShowHex="false"</Code>. For a value the user can see but not change, set <Code>ReadOnly</Code>; it still posts. For a value that doesn't post, set <Code>Disabled</Code>.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="color-picker-states" />
<List>
    <ListItem>A read-only picker still shows its hex box, because that's the part that can be focused.</ListItem>
    <ListItem>A read-only color posts from a hidden field. Before trusting a posted value, see <Anchor Href="docs/common/forms#values-a-form-post-can-change">Values a form post can change</Anchor>.</ListItem>
    <ListItem>A disabled picker posts nothing. After a form post on static pages, its property keeps the value a new model starts with. Before saving a disabled value from a form post, see <Anchor Href="docs/common/forms#disabled-fields-and-form-posts">Disabled fields and form posts</Anchor>.</ListItem>
</List>

<Heading Level="2">Labels and localization</Heading>
<TextBlock>The hex box, the panel's parts and the color names screen readers hear are all text you can set. To set them for the whole app, see <Anchor Href="docs/common/text">Text and other languages</Anchor>.</TextBlock>
<Prose Class="site-table ib-scroll">
    <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Parameter</th><th>Sets</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td><Code>HexLabel</Code></td><td>The end of the hex box's name</td><td>"Brand color, hex"</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
</Prose>

